    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d857">

                <pb facs="0792" n="676" sameAs="frus:frus1915/pg676"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/14674.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to Special Agent
                    Silliman</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-03-23T17:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 23, 1915—5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Uninterrupted railway communication between Vera Cruz and Mexico City is of
                    utmost importance. Mexico City authorities have expressed willingness to
                    consider neutralization of Mexican Railways in order that passenger and mail
                    traffic may be resumed.<anchor n="21" xml:id="d857-f1"/> Take up with Carranza
                    and point out that his favorable consideration of plan would be universally
                    approved.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bryan.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d857-f1" n="21" xml:id="d857-f1-content">
                <p>See telegram of March 17 from the British Minister to Mexico.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
